---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE, PRE_RELEASE } from "../config";

const platforms = [
  { name: "Windows", note: "Windows 10 and 11 · installer", published: true },
  { name: "macOS", note: "Apple silicon · .dmg", published: true },
  { name: "Linux", note: "Support pending", published: false },
];
---

<Base
  title="Download"
  metaTitle="Download TermHQ — Windows and macOS"
  description="Download TermHQ for Windows or macOS. A keyboard-first GUI terminal for running AI coding agents. Linux support is pending."
>
  <article class="page prose" data-pagefind-body>
    <h1 data-pagefind-meta="title">Download</h1>
    <p>
      TermHQ builds are published on GitHub Releases, each one built from an
      exact tagged source commit rather than from a moving branch — so a given
      version always means the same code.
    </p>

    {
      PRE_RELEASE && (
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            <strong>TermHQ has not been released yet.</strong> The links below
            will start working with the first public release. Nothing is
            downloadable today.
          </p>
        </blockquote>
      )
    }

    <div class="dl-grid">
      {
        platforms.map((p) => (
          <div class="dl-cell">
            <h3>{p.name}</h3>
            <p>{p.note}</p>
            {p.published ? (
              <a class="btn btn-primary" href={SITE.latestRelease}>
                Latest release
              </a>
            ) : null}
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <p>
      Those links resolve through GitHub's <code>releases/latest</code> redirect
      rather than naming a version, so they never point at a stale build and this
      page never needs editing when a release ships.
    </p>

    <h2 id="installing">Installing</h2>
    <p>
      Pre-release builds are <strong>not yet code-signed</strong>, so Windows
      and macOS each warn the first time you run one. That is the OS saying
      "unknown publisher" — not a fault it found. See
      <a href="/docs/installation/">Installation</a> for exactly what each
      platform shows, the steps past it, and
      <a href="/docs/installation/#why-the-install-warnings">why the warnings
      exist</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="updating">Updating</h2>
    <p>
      Download the current release and install over the top; your settings,
      themes and saved workspaces are left alone.
    </p>
    <p>
      TermHQ checks for updates shortly after launch and every six hours.
      Choose <strong>Update and restart</strong> from the update notification or
      Settings → General → Updates when you are ready. Running commands and
      agents stop during installation; your workspace and settings return.
      <a href="/docs/installation/#updating">Read the update and save guidance</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
